#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Validate the rubrical precedence sources and emit them for the browser.

Usage: calendar-rubrics {check,structure,assign} [--calendar NAME] ...

What this is, and why it is separate from `calendar-days`
---------------------------------------------------------
`calendar-days` refuses to rank. It says so in its own output: no general table
of precedence is stated in `guidance/liturgy/calendar-computation.md`, so a date
carrying several candidates emits them all with `precedence.stated: false`. That
refusal was right, and this tool does not overturn it. It supplies the missing
authority as a separate tracked source that can be read, checked and disagreed
with on its own, and leaves the day files exactly as they were.

The authority is not invented here either. Both `rubrics.yaml` files transcribe
rules from this repository's own collated publications -- the 1962 leaf read the
whole code as 260 dpi page images of a tracked facsimile and recorded every rule
in a rubric index with an evidence class; the postconciliar leaf read the
Universal Norms and the Table of Liturgical Days complete. Each source names its
publication, its sections and its witnesses, and records where it departs from
them.

Three things this tool refuses to do
------------------------------------
*It does not classify a mass the source has no rule for.* Every mass in a
calendar's index must match exactly one assignment rule; an unmatched mass is a
hard failure rather than a silent `unknown`, because a calendar that grows an
entry must surface here instead of vanishing into a default.

*It does not check the derivation in a second implementation.* The browser owns
the derivation, in `src/web/browser/liturgy/assembly-model.js`, and `check` runs
that same file under node against the solved cases each source carries. A Python
re-implementation would drift from the page it is meant to hold.

*It does not date what the calendar guidance leaves undated.* The Ember Days of
Advent and September are not placed by `calendar-days`, so a weekday that could
be one is emitted with `certain: false` and the reason, and the page says the
result may not hold.
"""
